\begin_layout Abstract
Football match results depend on a complex interaction between team performance and competitive dynamics.
 In this paper,
 we apply a series of machine learning methods and Isotonic Calibration to predict match outcomes using a dataset of around 230,000 football matches.
 Bookmaker odds were adjusted with the Newton–Raphson method to account for bookmaker margin effects,
 while additional calibration procedures were used to improve probability estimation.
 The proposed machine learning models achieved an AUC of 70.71% in predicting final match outcomes (1-X-2),
 with particularly strong performance in away-win predictions,
 reaching an accuracy of 72.02%.
 Compared to traditional scoring approaches such as Over/Under goals,
 the proposed method produced more consistent results across different leagues.
 The analysis also showed that combining statistical match features with calibrated machine learning methods can improve prediction reliability in highly competitive environments.
 
\end_layout

